1
00:00:01,830 --> 00:00:05,950
从微生物到参天大树
From the smallest microbe to the tallest tree,

2
00:00:06,070 --> 00:00:09,760
一看便知，这是生命
we think we know life when we see it.

3
00:00:09,870 --> 00:00:14,170
生命能否以异乎寻常的尺度存在
But could life also exist on a vastly different scale...

4
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:19,830
星球扮演着细胞的角色
...where planets act like single cells,

5
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:24,640
黑洞复制着空间的DNA
and black holes reproduce the DNA of space itself?

6
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:28,310
宇宙的秘密也许不在于物理学
Could the secrets of the cosmos lie not in physics,

7
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:30,740
而在生物学
but in biology?

8
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:34,480
宇宙是不是活着？
Is the Universe alive?

9
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:47,400
《同摩根·弗里曼一起穿越虫洞》
空间，时间，生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

10
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:55,180
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

11
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:03,410
宇宙是不是活着？

12
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:09,410
是什么使得你我活着？
What makes you and me alive?

13
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:14,180
什么使我们不同于岩石或机器人？
What makes us different from a rock or a robot?

14
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:16,490
心脏的跳动？
Is it the beating of our hearts?

15
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,620
头脑中竞相飘过的想法？
The thoughts that race through our heads?

16
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:24,060
或者我们出生、成长、死亡？
Or is it the fact that we are born, we grow, and we die?

17
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:25,760
现在，一些科学家认为
Now some scientists think

18
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:28,600
我们可能同比自身大得多的某些东西
we might share these fundamentals of life

19
00:01:28,710 --> 00:01:31,500
共享这些生命的基础
with something much larger than ourselves.

20
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:38,990
整个宇宙能否是活着的单一机体？
Could the entire cosmos be a single, living organism?

21
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:45,050
我小时候，在夏夜
When I was a kid, on summer nights,

22
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:46,420
我时常仰望
I would often look up

23
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:51,790
并捕捉闪烁的流萤
and catch the drifting, flickering glow of fireflies.

24
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,860
它们是活着的光点
They were living points of light.

25
00:01:55,970 --> 00:01:59,200
透过流萤，我看到满天繁星
Beyond them, I saw the stars, too,

26
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:04,100
在密西西比昏暗的天际眨着眼睛
flicker in the dark Mississippi sky.

27
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:08,070
它们就像活了一样
Something about them also seemed living.

28
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:10,870
想像一个巨大的生物体
To imagine a single living organism

29
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:13,540
其包绕寰宇
encompassing the entire Universe,

30
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,710
包括一望无垠的天际
including vast stretches of empty space,

31
00:02:16,830 --> 00:02:18,480
似乎违背逻辑
appears to defy logic.

32
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:23,920
但在抛弃这个想法之前，务必问问自己
But before you dismiss the idea, you have to ask yourself,

33
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:26,660
“活着”究竟意味着什么？
what does it really mean to be alive?

34
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:30,460
其实，这个问题并不简单
In fact, that question has no simple answer.

35
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:32,500
什么是生命？
What is life?

36
00:02:32,610 --> 00:02:36,370
没有令人信服的准确定义
There's no convincing definition that everybody agrees upon,

37
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:37,800
但大多数人认为
but most people say,

38
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:41,710
如果活着，就要能复制
if it is alive, then it has to replicate itself.

39
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:43,910
并能随时间进化
It has to evolve over time.

40
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:48,180
然而，某些科技产品也能复制
However, there are technological devices that also replicate

41
00:02:48,290 --> 00:02:50,450
也能随时间进化
and evolve over time.

42
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:52,480
例如，汽车
For example, cars.

43
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:55,790
汽车在工厂中
Cars replicate in car factories

44
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,120
按照一套指令复制
according to a set of instructions --

45
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:00,420
这指令有点像细胞中
a little bit like the DNA information

46
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:03,430
携带的DNA信息
that everybody carries in their cells.

47
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:07,300
如果只看定义
It turns out that if you look at these definitions,

48
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:09,830
就得承认汽车是活的
then you will have to admit that even cars are alive.

49
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:13,300
尤根·施密德胡贝尔
Jurgen Schmidhuber is a Professor

50
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:17,040
是瑞士卢加诺的一位人工智能教授
of artificial intelligence in Lugano, Switzerland.

51
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:20,710
他的目标是将我们对生命的定义
His goal is to push our definitions of life

52
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:22,250
推向新的尖端
to new frontiers.

53
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:26,150
他认为某些机器人应该被认为是活的
He believes that some robots should be considered alive.

54
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:29,120
他还认为单一生命体
He also believes that a single living organism

55
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:31,960
能拓展并超越一个躯体
can be spread out over more than one body.

56
00:03:32,070 --> 00:03:34,190
这群微型机器人
This swarm of tiny robots

57
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:39,030
通过编程，就像一个群落中的蚂蚁一样
is programmed to work together like ants in a colony

58
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:41,730
单一的实体，集体的大脑
as a single entity whose collective brain

59
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:45,240
分布在许多独立的躯体中
is distributed among many separate bodies --

60
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:48,200
“超有机体”
a "super-organism."

61
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:50,870
超有机体可以共同解决
Super-organisms can collectively solve problems

62
00:03:50,990 --> 00:03:54,540
单独一只蚂蚁解决不了的问题。
that none of the individual ants could solve.

63
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:57,250
而这样的“群机器人”
And swarm bots like these collections

64
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:01,550
每一个都被编程，遵循非常简单的规则
of robots each being programmed according to very simple rules,

65
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:03,520
它们可以共同解决
they can collectively solve problems

66
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:05,860
单独一个机器人解决不了的问题
that none of the individual robots can solve.

67
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:09,630
蚂蚁通过分泌 信息素 来寻找食物发现威胁。
Ants find food and signal the presence of danger

68
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:11,530
这种信息素
by secreting pheromones

69
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:15,030
可以改变其他蚂蚁行为。
that change the behavior of other ants.

70
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:17,430
不同于化学信息
Instead of chemicals,

71
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:21,740
群机器人交换简单的电子信息
swarm bots exchange simple electronic messages.

72
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:24,370
当遇到障碍物时
Whenever it comes close to some obstacle,

73
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:27,410
它稍稍转动自己的旋转角
then it shifts its rotation angle a little bit

74
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:29,210
并离开
and moves away.

75
00:04:29,330 --> 00:04:32,150
当两三个机器人相撞
And whenever two or three swarm bots collide

76
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:33,950
或者彼此靠近时
or come close to each other,

77
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,320
它们交换一点信息
they exchange a bit of information.

78
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:39,820
例如，你能让它们站队（集中在特定的群体中）
For example, you can make them collect in certain clusters.

79
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:41,960
也能让它们通过
You can also make them clean rooms

80
00:04:42,070 --> 00:04:45,300
将房间中发现的障碍物推到特定角落
by pushing all the obstacles that you find in this room

81
00:04:45,410 --> 00:04:46,830
来打扫房间
into a certain corner.

82
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:51,130
所以许多简单的规则 被认为
So many, many simple rules can be thought of

83
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:53,540
可形成复杂的新表现
that form a complex emerging behavior

84
00:04:53,650 --> 00:04:55,910
并产生其他惊人的结果
that leads to other, amazing results.

85
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:58,440
如果宇宙是活的，
If the Universe is alive,

86
00:04:58,550 --> 00:05:03,480
尤根相信那一定也是一个“超有机体”
jurgen believes it also must be a super-organism,

87
00:05:03,590 --> 00:05:06,280
分布于横亘无数光年的空间中
spread out across the countless light years of space

88
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:09,990
像宇宙蚁群
like a cosmic colony of ants.

89
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:12,900
“超有机体”能有多大？
But how big can a super-organism get?

90
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:22,830
杰弗里·韦斯特 教授是杰出的粒子物理学家
Professor Geoffrey West is a distinguished particle physicist

91
00:05:22,950 --> 00:05:24,970
效力于圣达菲研究所
at the Santa Fe Institute.

92
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:29,140
他确信，巨大的生命体就在我们身边
He believes giant, living beings are all around us.

93
00:05:29,250 --> 00:05:33,240
事实上，我们当中多数人都住在里面
In fact, most of us live inside them.

94
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:37,480
我认为生命的本质特征在于其复杂的系统
I think an essential feature of life is its complex system,

95
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:41,280
即它由大量部件组成
which means it's made up of enormous numbers of components.

96
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:44,820
在此之下，是新陈代谢的概念
But underlying all of that is this idea of metabolism,

97
00:05:44,930 --> 00:05:51,190
用能量、资源来驱动系统
in which you use energy, resources to drive the system.

98
00:05:51,310 --> 00:05:54,530
城市 也是这样的
And we see that reflected in cities.

99
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:56,700
如同
In much the same way

100
00:05:56,810 --> 00:05:58,700
人类的身体这样的机体
that an organism like the human body

101
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:02,910
由细胞、骨骼及血管组成
is made of cells, bones, and blood vessels,

102
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:05,080
洛杉矶城这样的超机体
a super-organism like the city of Los Angeles

103
00:06:05,190 --> 00:06:09,750
由人口、公路、输电线组成
is made of people, highways, and power lines.

104
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:12,250
和生物机体一样
And just like any biological organism,

105
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:14,880
能量流经城市的身躯
energy flows through the body of a city.

106
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,160
城市有许多生物体的特点
Cities have many of the characteristics of organisms.

107
00:06:21,270 --> 00:06:24,430
有同生物体相似网状结构
There are network structures like organisms.

108
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:26,230
事物流经其间
They have flows in them --

109
00:06:26,340 --> 00:06:28,630
从车流、人群
everything from automobiles and people

110
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:30,900
到电力和资源
to electricity and other resources.

111
00:06:31,010 --> 00:06:35,610
正如我们具有的 循环系统
Just as organisms do, we have, of course, circulatory systems

112
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:38,480
呼吸系统 等等
and respiratory systems and so on.

113
00:06:38,590 --> 00:06:41,180
近年来，杰弗里致力于
Geoffrey has spent the last few years

114
00:06:41,290 --> 00:06:43,210
创立一个数学系统
working out a mathematical system

115
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:46,520
来预测 若要活着，需要多少能量
that predicts how much energy must flow through a being

116
00:06:46,630 --> 00:06:47,750
流经躯体
for it to be alive,

117
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:50,590
以及能量和生命形态的关系
and how that energy shapes its life.

118
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:53,420
已知哺乳动物身体的大小
If you tell me the size of a mammal,

119
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:56,630
就可得到其代谢速率
I can tell you its metabolic rate.

120
00:06:56,740 --> 00:06:58,930
其心律、寿命
Its heart rate, how long it's gonna live,

121
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:02,000
其后代的数量
how many offspring it will have.

122
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:03,430
这一切之下
Underlying all this

123
00:07:03,550 --> 00:07:06,570
是 网络 的数学、物理原理
is the mathematics and physics of networks.

124
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:09,010
明白了
Seeing living creatures

125
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:12,280
生物不过是分配能量的网络
as nothing more than networks that distribute energy

126
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:14,410
杰弗里就能据此可以计算出
allows Geoffrey to calculate

127
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,680
小型啮齿类大小的动物
that an animal the size of a small rodent

128
00:07:17,790 --> 00:07:21,920
需要大约500次每分钟的心律
should have a heart rate at around 500 beats per minute.

129
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:29,430
而鲸的心脏只需要10秒钟搏动一次
Whereas a whale heart only pulses once every 10 seconds.

130
00:07:29,540 --> 00:07:33,660
鲸的体重大约是鼩鼱的一亿倍
A whale is about 100 million times as heavy as a shrew.

131
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:35,270
而且，基本上
And yet, basically,

132
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:39,370
我们都是相同事物的 不同比例大小的版本
we're all the scaled versions of the same thing.

133
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:45,010
由此可得一个很自然的问题
So it's a very natural question that follows from that is,

134
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:46,940
城市是不是也这样？
does any of this work for cities?

135
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:52,320
根据杰弗里的计算结果，答案是肯定的
The answer, according to Geoffrey's calculations, is yes.

136
00:07:52,430 --> 00:07:55,250
城市沿着其街道和输电线
Cities distribute energy around their networks of streets

137
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,650
分配能量
and power lines

138
00:07:56,770 --> 00:08:01,060
遵循着和生物体非常相似的规则
according to the very same rules as biological beings.

139
00:08:01,170 --> 00:08:02,760
城市是如此之大
And cities are so large

140
00:08:02,870 --> 00:08:05,830
伴随着上下班往返的人潮
that their hearts pulse only twice a day,

141
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:09,430
其心律仅为一天两次
with the ebb and flow of commuters.

142
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:12,000
我们独特的大动脉
We have this extraordinary artery

143
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:15,070
直通洛杉矶的市中心
coming through the center of Los Angeles,

144
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,210
洛杉矶的心脏
the heart of Los Angeles.

145
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:18,710
然后
And indeed, if you look up,

146
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:21,710
仰望这些非凡的建筑
you see these extraordinary buildings

147
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:24,750
它们在许多方面扮演着心脏的角色
which, in many ways, acting as a heart

148
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:27,820
因为它们代表财富的创造
because they represent wealth creation,

149
00:08:27,930 --> 00:08:30,190
当然，它是一个泵
and that, of course, is a pump

150
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:33,320
带动了交通
which helps pump a lot of this traffic.

151
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:36,160
洛杉矶这样的城市
A city like Los Angeles

152
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:39,500
是一个巨型尺度下的生命实体
is a living entity on a colossal scale,

153
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:43,800
四十英里宽，随着能量缓缓搏动
40 miles wide, slowly pulsing with energy.

154
00:08:43,910 --> 00:08:46,370
但没有城市是孤立的
But no city lives in isolation.

155
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:49,810
城市遍及我们整个星球表面
Our entire planet is covered with them.

156
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:52,880
所以它们具有多重尺度
There are, of course, multiple scales.

157
00:08:52,990 --> 00:08:55,710
我们可以从城市尺度
You know, we can go from the scale of city

158
00:08:55,830 --> 00:09:00,180
扩展到国家尺度，星球尺度
to the scale of a country to the scale of the planet.

159
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:05,560
杰弗里·韦斯特已经学会了从多重尺度观察生命
Geoffrey West has learned how to see life on many scales.

160
00:09:05,670 --> 00:09:07,820
当生命体聚集在一起时
When living beings come together,

161
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:12,830
无论是蚂蚁、人类或整个大城市
no matter whether they're ants, humans, or entire metropolises,

162
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:16,570
它们形成的整体都表现出生命的特征
what they create takes on a life of its own.

163
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:20,940
这一观点能否推广并超越地球？
But can this concept make the leap beyond our planet?

164
00:09:21,050 --> 00:09:25,040
一个“超机体”能否跨越山峦大洋
Can a super-organism span not just mountain ranges and oceans,

165
00:09:25,150 --> 00:09:28,580
并扩展到广袤的外太空？
but also vast expanses of outer space?

166
00:09:28,690 --> 00:09:31,010
一名物理学家认为有可能
One physicist thinks it might,

167
00:09:31,130 --> 00:09:33,320
他相信他已发现了
and he believes he has discovered

168
00:09:33,430 --> 00:09:37,100
宇宙的心跳
the heartbeat of the cosmos.

169
00:09:39,450 --> 00:09:43,810
我们的心脏大约每秒跳动一次
About once every second, our hearts take a beat.

170
00:09:43,930 --> 00:09:45,250
每次搏动
With each pulse,

171
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:49,390
都将氧、液体和能量带给细胞
they deliver oxygen, fluid, and energy to our cells.

172
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,350
倘若宇宙也有心脏
If the Universe had a heart,

173
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:54,120
像空间泵送物质和能量
pumping matter and energy into space,

174
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:58,410
我们能否觉察到它的搏动
could we detect its pulse?

175
00:10:02,740 --> 00:10:07,040
斯蒂芬·亚历山大 出生于 特立尼达
Stephon Alexander was born in Trinidad

176
00:10:07,150 --> 00:10:09,510
在 布朗克斯 长大
and raised in the Bronx.

177
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:12,380
作为萨克斯手，他能够分辨
As a saxophonist, he learned to find the melody

178
00:10:12,490 --> 00:10:15,780
贯穿于爵士副歌嘈杂音符中的旋律
that threads through the chaotic notes of a jazz refrain.

179
00:10:15,890 --> 00:10:19,480
在理论物理学界，斯蒂芬相信他能
And through theoretical physics, Stephon believes he is learning

180
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:23,620
听到宇宙基本的节拍/心跳
to hear the fundamental beat of the Universe.

181
00:10:23,730 --> 00:10:25,450
宇宙可能是生物体
It's a wonderful thought to ponder

182
00:10:25,570 --> 00:10:27,620
这是个好想法
that the Universe could be a living thing.

183
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:29,990
我是说，甚至生物学自身
I mean, even in biology itself,

184
00:10:30,110 --> 00:10:32,600
生物学家们还在纠结
biologists are struggling with the definition

185
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:34,960
生命的定义和起源
and the origin of life itself.

186
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:37,330
但应有可信的实验
But there should be certain experiments

187
00:10:37,450 --> 00:10:38,670
来寻找
that look for patterns

188
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:41,250
关乎生命功能的模式
that pertain to the function of a living thing.

189
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:49,780
斯蒂芬的研究 可能引领我们
Stephon's research may be leading us

190
00:10:49,890 --> 00:10:52,480
从宇宙自身 找到这些模式
towards finding those patterns in the cosmos itself.

191
00:10:52,590 --> 00:10:56,350
大多数宇宙学家相信我们的宇宙
While most cosmologists believe our Universe was created

192
00:10:56,470 --> 00:10:59,620
诞生于大爆炸
in an explosive beginning called the Big Bang,

193
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:04,990
斯蒂芬相信，大爆炸实际上是大弹跳
Stephon believes the Big Bang was actually a big bounce.

194
00:11:05,110 --> 00:11:08,700
大弹跳模型，简而言之
The big bounce model is, simply put,

195
00:11:08,810 --> 00:11:11,370
是一个早期宇宙的模型
a model of the early Universe.

196
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:15,040
事实上，前一个宇宙
In fact, there was a previous Universe

197
00:11:15,150 --> 00:11:17,240
坍缩之后
that underwent collapse

198
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:19,440
反弹成一个膨胀的宇宙
and bounced out into and expand the Universe,

199
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:21,340
即我们栖息的宇宙
which is the Universe that we inhabit.

200
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:23,880
斯蒂芬的理论中
In Stephon's theory,

201
00:11:23,990 --> 00:11:27,120
膨胀的宇宙只是
the expanding Universe is just one phase

202
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:31,220
无尽的坍缩与膨胀循环中的一瞥
in a never-ending cycle of contraction and expansion,

203
00:11:31,330 --> 00:11:32,990
就像心跳
like a beating heart,

204
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:37,290
像掉下又弹起的球
or like the falling and rising of a bouncing handball.

205
00:11:37,410 --> 00:11:39,200
我们观察会发生什么
So let's observe what happens.

206
00:11:39,310 --> 00:11:40,500
将球掷下
I drop the ball.

207
00:11:40,610 --> 00:11:43,400
重力会使球掉落
Gravity's going to, of course, pull the ball down.

208
00:11:43,510 --> 00:11:47,110
接着球会弹起
And what happens is that the ball bounces up.

209
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:53,540
弹跳的宇宙
So likewise, the bounced Universe,

210
00:11:53,660 --> 00:11:55,710
在万有引力的支配下 也会这样做
under the laws of gravity, will do a similar thing.

211
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:59,780
它会收缩，然后反弹会膨胀的状态
It contracts, and it can bounce back into an expanded phase.

212
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:04,950
斯蒂芬的模型大都包含着简单的物理学
Most of Stephon's model involves simple physics.

213
00:12:05,070 --> 00:12:07,660
膨胀，迟早都会
The expanding phase will, sooner or later,

214
00:12:07,770 --> 00:12:11,430
由于克服引力而精疲力竭
run out of steam, overcome by the attractive force of gravity,

215
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:14,000
引力最终将会把宇宙万物
which eventually pulls everything in the Universe

216
00:12:14,110 --> 00:12:17,000
压缩到很小的尺度
down to a tiny size.

217
00:12:17,110 --> 00:12:20,870
但解释 为什么宇宙会从这一点回复元气而反弹
But explaining why the Universe bounces back from this point

218
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:23,370
却复杂得多
is far more complex.

219
00:12:23,490 --> 00:12:26,180
弹跳宇宙模型的一个问题
One of the problems with the bounce in cosmology

220
00:12:26,290 --> 00:12:27,910
在于反弹时
is that you still have to ask a question.

221
00:12:28,020 --> 00:12:32,350
究竟发生了什么？重力变的非常强
What happens when you bounce? Gravity becomes very strong.

222
00:12:32,460 --> 00:12:35,250
重力相互作用占主导地位
Okay, the gravitational interaction dominates.

223
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:38,090
那会驱使我们成为奇点
That, in fact, would drive us into the singularity.

224
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:42,490
奇点是具有无限大密度的地方
A singularity is a place of infinite density --

225
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:45,060
那里的重力之强
a place where gravity becomes so strong

226
00:12:45,170 --> 00:12:48,030
以至于把所有质量压缩到一点
that it crushes all matter into a single point,

227
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:53,600
不允许任何事物逃脱 光也不例外
allowing nothing to escape, not even light.

228
00:12:53,720 --> 00:12:57,770
那些宇宙学家相信大爆炸理论
Those cosmologists who believe in the Big Bang theory

229
00:12:57,890 --> 00:13:00,040
相信曾经是一个奇点
believe it was a singularity,

230
00:13:00,160 --> 00:13:03,650
他们必须发明一种新的力 称为暴涨
and they had to invent a new force called inflation

231
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:08,020
使宇宙膨胀脱离它
to allow the Universe to expand out from it.

232
00:13:08,130 --> 00:13:11,490
另一方面 斯蒂芬认为大弹跳宇宙
Stephon, on the other hand, thinks the Big Bounce Universe

233
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:14,860
从未收缩到一个奇点
never shrinks down to a singularity.

234
00:13:14,970 --> 00:13:18,390
当宇宙到达反弹点的时候
As the Universe approaches the bounce point,

235
00:13:18,510 --> 00:13:21,660
诚然 这规避了大爆炸奇点
it has to, of course, avoid that big bang singularity.

236
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:23,100
我们规避了这个问题
So we have to avoid that problem.

237
00:13:23,210 --> 00:13:26,040
斯蒂芬认为 这个解
The solution, Stephon believes,

238
00:13:26,150 --> 00:13:29,870
由一种比原子小得多的粒子所提供
is provided by a tiny particle far smaller than an atom

239
00:13:29,990 --> 00:13:33,910
即中微子
known as the neutrino.

240
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:36,250
中微子 属于物理学中已知的
Neutrinos, which are some of the strangest particles

241
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:37,450
最奇怪的粒子
known in physics,

242
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:39,420
会在理解弹跳宇宙场景上
could play a major role

243
00:13:39,530 --> 00:13:43,120
扮演主要角色
in understanding the bouncing universe scenario.

244
00:13:43,230 --> 00:13:46,020
每时每刻 中微子都在
A neutrino can, just at that moment,

245
00:13:46,130 --> 00:13:48,020
提供斥力
have a repulsive force

246
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:50,820
使你不至于被压缩成奇点
that prevents you from crunching into the singularity

247
00:13:50,930 --> 00:13:51,930
并反弹
and bounce out.

248
00:13:52,050 --> 00:13:56,400
中微子几乎没有质量
Neutrinos have almost no mass,

249
00:13:56,510 --> 00:14:00,770
悄无声息地穿过普通物质
and they pass through ordinary matter undetected.

250
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:05,310
每秒钟有五十万亿个这样的幽灵粒子
50 trillion of these ghostly particles

251
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:08,280
正在移动 穿过我们的身体
move right through each of our bodies every second

252
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:09,910
却未被察觉
without our ever knowing it.

253
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:11,480
宇宙中的每颗恒星
Every star in the Universe

254
00:14:11,590 --> 00:14:15,680
喷涌出恒定的中微子流
spews out a constant stream of neutrinos.

255
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:19,090
事实上，宇宙间塞满了它们
In fact, the Universe is chock full of them,

256
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:22,490
可能正是它们 赋予了宇宙自身
and they may be what gives the living universe

257
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:25,360
稳定的脉搏
its steady pulse.

258
00:14:25,470 --> 00:14:28,260
斯蒂芬计算了
Stephon has calculated

259
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:32,130
在宇宙压缩的巨大压力下
that under the immense pressure of a contracting Universe,

260
00:14:32,250 --> 00:14:34,670
遍布宇宙的中微子海洋
this cosmic sea of neutrinos

261
00:14:34,780 --> 00:14:37,440
会被紧紧压缩在一起
would become so tightly squeezed together,

262
00:14:37,550 --> 00:14:40,510
成为物质的一种特殊形式
they'd transform into a special state of matter

263
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:42,480
超流体
called a superfluid.

264
00:14:42,590 --> 00:14:46,420
我们都知道超流体态
A superfluid state is well-known to us.

265
00:14:46,530 --> 00:14:49,080
液氦是超流体的一个例子
Liquid helium is an example of a superfluid.

266
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:51,720
超流体的一个奇异的特性在于
One of the strange properties of a superfluid

267
00:14:51,830 --> 00:14:54,090
如果试图收集它
is that, if you try to contain it,

268
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:58,490
它仍会从容器的边缘流走
it will still flow out, out of the edges of the container.

269
00:14:58,610 --> 00:15:00,860
它不喜欢被收集
It doesn't like to be contained.

270
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:04,500
中微子不被收容的趋势
This tendency for a neutrino to not be contained

271
00:15:04,610 --> 00:15:07,500
产生排斥的压力
causes a repulsive pressure

272
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:11,790
使得宇宙反弹并膨胀
and causes the Universe to bounce and expand.

273
00:15:16,890 --> 00:15:18,980
如果斯蒂芬的想法是对的
If Stephon's idea is right,

274
00:15:19,090 --> 00:15:21,480
我们的宇宙已经在
our Universe has been going through cycles

275
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:25,050
膨胀和压缩的循环中徘徊了万亿年
of expansion and contraction for trillions of years.

276
00:15:25,170 --> 00:15:26,790
每次反弹
At each bounce,

277
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:30,490
大多数物质 包括星球和星系
most of the matter, including any stars or galaxies,

278
00:15:30,610 --> 00:15:35,660
都被压碎 然后以全新的形式喷出
is crushed and then spat out in completely new form.

279
00:15:35,780 --> 00:15:38,300
但是中微子却好发无损
But neutrinos survive unscathed.

280
00:15:38,410 --> 00:15:40,840
这一不可见的粒子海洋
This invisible sea of particles

281
00:15:40,950 --> 00:15:43,670
是宇宙生命的血液
is the lifeblood of the Universe,

282
00:15:43,790 --> 00:15:46,140
驱动脉搏的流体
the fluid that drives its pulse.

283
00:15:46,250 --> 00:15:49,880
或者 中微子驱动宇宙
Or, if you prefer, neutrinos propel the cosmos

284
00:15:49,990 --> 00:15:53,520
从其生命周期的这一轮到下一轮
from one phase of its life cycle to the next.

285
00:15:53,630 --> 00:15:56,190
大弹跳的一种类比
A good analogy for the big bounce

286
00:15:56,300 --> 00:15:59,520
就像毛毛虫成为蝴蝶
is like a caterpillar becoming a butterfly.

287
00:15:59,630 --> 00:16:03,960
从毛虫变成蝴蝶
You have the caterpillar that emerges as a butterfly

288
00:16:04,070 --> 00:16:06,660
看起来完全不同
that looks completely different,

289
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:08,400
但有着相同的遗传密码
but has the same genetic code

290
00:16:08,510 --> 00:16:10,530
并由相同的东西组成
and is made up of the same stuff.

291
00:16:10,650 --> 00:16:14,070
生物学变态的类比
So in that sense, the analogy from biology of metamorphosis,

292
00:16:14,180 --> 00:16:18,440
我认为在这里相当适用
I think, is quite applicable here and worth pursuing.

293
00:16:18,550 --> 00:16:20,940
斯蒂芬的大弹跳模型
Stephon's big bounce model

294
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:25,710
将宇宙描绘为动态的 进化中的实体
portrays our cosmos as a dynamic, evolving entity --

295
00:16:25,830 --> 00:16:28,380
可以是活着的什么东西
something that could be alive.

296
00:16:28,500 --> 00:16:33,020
另一位科学家将这一想法进一步深化
And another scientist has taken this idea one step further.

297
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:34,560
他认为我们宇宙
He thinks our Universe

298
00:16:34,670 --> 00:16:38,230
产生了一家子后代宇宙
has given birth to a whole family of cosmic offspring --

299
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:39,900
那些深藏在
universes that lay hidden

300
00:16:40,010 --> 00:16:44,340
黑洞昏暗视界后面的宇宙
behind the dark horizons of black holes.

301
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:52,530
所有生物具有一个共同特征
All living things share one common feature --

302
00:16:52,650 --> 00:16:55,170
它们都来自于某物
they've all come from something.

303
00:16:55,280 --> 00:16:58,140
橡树来自于
The oak tree rises from the acorn

304
00:16:58,250 --> 00:17:00,610
另一棵橡树上掉下的橡果
which fell from another oak.

305
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:05,610
人类的家谱可回溯数千代
The human family tree dates back many thousands of generations.

306
00:17:05,730 --> 00:17:10,450
如果宇宙是活的，它有没有祖先？
If our Universe is alive, does it have an ancestor?

307
00:17:10,560 --> 00:17:16,560
它能不能孕育其他宇宙
And could it give birth to other universes?

308
00:17:16,670 --> 00:17:20,560
李·斯莫林
Lee Smolin conducts research

309
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:23,160
工作于 周界理论物理研究院
at the Perimeter Institute for Theoretical Physics

310
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:25,330
滑铁卢，加拿大
in Waterloo, Canada.

311
00:17:25,450 --> 00:17:26,900
李在最基础的层面上
Lee thinks about the laws of physics

312
00:17:27,010 --> 00:17:30,000
思考物理定律
at the most fundamental level.

313
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:34,080
他试图了解我们的宇宙为何
He's trying to understand how our Universe ended up

314
00:17:34,190 --> 00:17:36,580
具有这些特定的物理定律
with the particular laws of physics it has.

315
00:17:36,690 --> 00:17:39,710
自然法则似乎是被精心调谐
The laws of nature appear to be fine-tuned

316
00:17:39,830 --> 00:17:42,880
所以宇宙才能孕育生命
so that the Universe is hospitable to life.

317
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:44,550
如果考虑
If you consider what would happen

318
00:17:44,670 --> 00:17:47,090
稍稍调整自然法则的后果
if you change the laws of nature even slightly,

319
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:49,920
宇宙便不会对生命友好
then the Universe would cease to be friendly to life.

320
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:51,530
所以
So there is something --

321
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:55,260
宇宙之所以宜居 它为什么对生命如此友好
there's a mystery about why the Universe is so hospitable,

322
00:17:55,380 --> 00:17:57,330
是一个谜
why it's so friendly to biology.

323
00:17:57,450 --> 00:18:01,000
宇宙学家努力揭开
Cosmologists have long wrestled with this puzzle

324
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:04,140
他们称为恰当调谐的谜底
which they call fine tuning.

325
00:18:04,250 --> 00:18:06,470
如果某种自然的力
If any of the forces of nature

326
00:18:06,590 --> 00:18:09,980
更强一些，或者更弱一点，哪怕1%
were stronger or weaker by a fraction of 1%,

327
00:18:10,090 --> 00:18:13,050
星球和星系歇菜了
stars and galaxies would never form.

328
00:18:13,160 --> 00:18:15,450
甚至原子也将不复存在
Even atoms might not exist.

329
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:19,190
这意味着，我们的宇宙
To some, this is a sign that our Universe

330
00:18:19,300 --> 00:18:23,190
经过了创世者精心的调整
was carefully crafted by the hand of a creator

331
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:27,200
创世者使之可以支持人类生命
who tailor-made it to support human life.

332
00:18:27,310 --> 00:18:33,470
但是李想要一个更科学的解释，他找到了一个
But Lee wanted a more scientific explanation, and he found one.

333
00:18:33,580 --> 00:18:34,800
不植根于物理
Not rooted in physics,

334
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:39,240
却在生物的进化理论
but in the biological theory of evolution.

335
00:18:39,350 --> 00:18:41,940
自然选择
In natural selection, you explain

336
00:18:42,060 --> 00:18:44,080
解释了错综复杂的生命结构
how while the intricacy and structure of life

337
00:18:44,190 --> 00:18:45,380
依次出现，
arises progressively,

338
00:18:45,490 --> 00:18:48,050
直到获得了非常难以置信的东西。
you have something which is very improbable.

339
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:50,390
这种方法可以解释。
There's a method to explain it.

340
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:55,660
进化论者认为，人类这样的复杂生物
Evolutionists believe that creatures as complex as humans

341
00:18:55,770 --> 00:19:00,290
并不是奇迹般突然冒出来的
did not just miraculously pop into existence.

342
00:19:00,410 --> 00:19:02,760
而是经过无数步骤，从更简单的机体
But rather, evolved through countless steps

343
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:05,570
进化得来
from ever-simpler organisms.

344
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:10,140
同理，李考虑我们难以置信的复杂宇宙
Likewise, Lee wondered whether our immensely complex universe

345
00:19:10,250 --> 00:19:15,580
正是通过生物进化得到的一个宇宙版本
was also shaped by a cosmic version of biological evolution.

346
00:19:15,690 --> 00:19:17,910
宇宙有没有历史
Could the Universe have a history?

347
00:19:18,030 --> 00:19:19,480
有没有祖先？
Could it have ancestors?

348
00:19:19,590 --> 00:19:23,350
它经过进化，从历史走来？
Has it evolved through its history?

349
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:26,590
定律是否经历过的随机变化，
Could there have been random variation of laws,

350
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:28,990
然后选择了那些定律，
and then selection of those laws,

351
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:31,560
那些具有最多结构
selecting those that introduced the most structure,

352
00:19:31,670 --> 00:19:32,690
最复杂的定律。
the most complexity.

353
00:19:32,810 --> 00:19:34,800
我问自己这些问题
So I asked myself that as a question,

354
00:19:34,910 --> 00:19:39,070
宇宙自然选择 是一个答案
and cosmological natural selection is one answer to it.

355
00:19:39,180 --> 00:19:41,300
也是迄今我知道的 最好的答案
It's the best answer to it so far, I know.

356
00:19:41,420 --> 00:19:44,740
但在认定李教授的
But in order for Lee's theory

357
00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:47,810
宇宙自然选择 理论站得住脚之前
of cosmological natural selection to work,

358
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:51,250
还得解决整个宇宙是如何
there has to be a mechanism by which an entire cosmos

359
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:54,250
复制并发生突变
can reproduce and undergo mutation,

360
00:19:54,360 --> 00:19:57,690
并将DNA又亲代传递到子代
the way DNA does from parent to child.

361
00:19:57,800 --> 00:19:59,520
即需要一种繁殖模型
So you need a mode of reproduction.

362
00:19:59,630 --> 00:20:00,790
需要 变异
You need variation.

363
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:03,460
需要一种东西
You need something that determines the properties

364
00:20:03,570 --> 00:20:05,530
来决定由亲代到子代
to vary from instance to instance,

365
00:20:05,640 --> 00:20:07,200
个体间不同的特征
from ancestor to child.

366
00:20:07,310 --> 00:20:09,200
此外还需要 选择
And then you need selection.

367
00:20:09,310 --> 00:20:13,070
这些东西是宇宙中的什么？
So what could be those elements in cosmology?

368
00:20:13,180 --> 00:20:15,670
这些问题的答案
The answer to all these questions,

369
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:20,680
李相信，隐藏在黑洞中心
Lee believes, lies in the hidden hearts of black holes,

370
00:20:20,790 --> 00:20:23,950
在那里 已知的物理定律失效
where the known laws of physics break down,

371
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:26,410
并由目前仍未完全了解的
and the as-yet-unknown laws

372
00:20:26,530 --> 00:20:30,620
称为“量子引力”的定律接管
of a theory called quantum gravity take over.

373
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:34,290
当巨大的恒星爆炸时 我们现有的理论预计
When a giant star explodes, our current theories predict

374
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:37,730
它的核心会坍缩到一个点
that its core should collapse down to a single point

375
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:39,730
并拥有无穷的密度
and become infinitely dense.

376
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:43,770
但是李对于量子引力开拓性地理解
But Lee's pioneering efforts to understand quantum gravity

377
00:20:43,880 --> 00:20:46,600
认为并不这样
suggest it does not,

378
00:20:46,710 --> 00:20:47,970
而是
and that instead,

379
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:51,110
此刻正是一个全新宇宙的诞生
this is the moment an entire new Universe is born.

380
00:20:51,220 --> 00:20:54,540
形成黑洞的恒星坍缩
The star that formed a black hole is collapsing,

381
00:20:54,660 --> 00:20:57,610
在即将拥有无限大密度之前
and just before it would become infinitely dense,

382
00:20:57,730 --> 00:21:00,110
它反弹 并再次开始膨胀
it bounces and begins expanding again.

383
00:21:00,230 --> 00:21:02,920
此刻就可在黑洞视界之内
And you can evolve new regions of space-time

384
00:21:03,030 --> 00:21:07,220
形成新的时-空区域
still within the confines of the horizon of the black hole.

385
00:21:07,330 --> 00:21:09,720
这一区域可以生长变大
But this region could grow and become large

386
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:12,130
正如我们宇宙在大爆炸之后那样
like our universe did after the Big Bang.

387
00:21:12,240 --> 00:21:18,970
这些新宇宙时空的维度
The dimensions of space and time of these new universes

388
00:21:19,080 --> 00:21:22,670
从黑洞内部与我们的宇宙分道扬镳
branch off from ours inside the black hole.

389
00:21:22,780 --> 00:21:25,510
这一切发生时 物理定律在这暴力一刻
The laws of physics change when that happens

390
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:26,970
发生改变
at this very violent event,

391
00:21:27,090 --> 00:21:30,110
所以亲代和子代相似
so that the parent resembles the children,

392
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:33,280
子代之间彼此相似
and the children's children resemble them,

393
00:21:33,390 --> 00:21:35,120
但存在小的改变
but there are small changes.

394
00:21:35,230 --> 00:21:37,120
就像生物学那样
It works just like biology.

395
00:21:37,230 --> 00:21:39,850
有了宇宙群体
You have a population of universes.

396
00:21:39,970 --> 00:21:43,720
他们通过黑洞产生后代
They give rise to progeny through black holes,

397
00:21:43,840 --> 00:21:47,830
如果说最成功的定律是什么
and if you ask what laws are most successful,

398
00:21:47,940 --> 00:21:49,200
就是那些
those are the laws

399
00:21:49,310 --> 00:21:52,000
能使宇宙产生最多的黑洞的定律
that lead to universes that have the most black holes.

400
00:21:52,110 --> 00:21:54,640
一代代下去 就得到了
So what you get, generation by generation,

401
00:21:54,750 --> 00:21:56,370
选择趋势
is selection for the tendency

402
00:21:56,480 --> 00:21:59,020
尽可能产生更多黑洞
to make as many black holes as possible.

403
00:22:05,220 --> 00:22:07,380
我们的宇宙可能
Our Universe might exist

404
00:22:07,490 --> 00:22:09,950
位于一个生生不息的宇宙家谱中
on an ever-growing cosmic tree of life.

405
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:13,520
这还不是李的
But this is not the most remarkable aspect

406
00:22:13,630 --> 00:22:17,760
宇宙自然选择理论最卓越的方面
of Lee's theory of cosmological natural selection.

407
00:22:17,870 --> 00:22:20,560
他探索了何种物理定律
As he explored which laws of physics

408
00:22:20,670 --> 00:22:24,130
能让宇宙产生更多的后代
allow a baby universe to produce more offspring,

409
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:26,200
他发现了宇宙家谱
he discovered an uncanny connection

410
00:22:26,310 --> 00:22:32,010
和我们的生物家谱之间 神秘的关联
between the cosmic tree of life and our own biological one.

411
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:35,680
为了产生黑洞 需要大质量的恒星
To make black holes, you need very massive stars.

412
00:22:35,790 --> 00:22:39,050
至少要太阳质量的20倍
You need stars of 20 times the mass of the sun or more.

413
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:40,750
为此
And to make these,

414
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:44,590
需要冷却大量的云气和尘埃
you need big clouds of gas and dust that are cooled.

415
00:22:44,700 --> 00:22:48,090
而冷却剂正是一氧化碳
So the coolant turns out to be carbon monoxide.

416
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:51,460
需要的碳和氧
And so you need carbon, and you need oxygen,

417
00:22:51,570 --> 00:22:55,160
正是生命需求最大的两种原子
which are the two atoms that you need most plentifully for life.

418
00:22:55,280 --> 00:22:58,330
事实上，拥有大量碳
And in fact, the universe has lots and lots of carbon

419
00:22:58,450 --> 00:23:00,370
和大量氧的宇宙
and lots and lots of oxygen,

420
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:04,010
就解释了为什么宇宙对生命友好
so the explanation for why the universe is bio-friendly

421
00:23:04,120 --> 00:23:10,120
生命友好恰恰是宇宙为了自我复制，为了多产
is a side effect of the universe being very fruitful

422
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:12,710
而出现的副产物
in terms of its own reproduction.

423
00:23:12,830 --> 00:23:14,480
我觉得这非常可爱
And I find that very endearing.

424
00:23:14,600 --> 00:23:16,520
如果李说的对
If Lee is correct,

425
00:23:16,630 --> 00:23:19,690
我们认为恰当调谐的物理定律
the laws of physics we know have been fine-tuned

426
00:23:19,800 --> 00:23:22,620
是宇宙为自我复制
in order to keep the cosmos fertile

427
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:25,490
而保持多产用的
for its own reproduction.

428
00:23:25,610 --> 00:23:28,860
相同的定律恰好使宇宙成为
Those same laws also happen to make our universe

429
00:23:28,980 --> 00:23:34,570
利于碳基生物繁衍生息的地方
a place where carbon-based life can flourish.

430
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:37,970
我们的宇宙可能只是
Our universe may be just one member

431
00:23:38,090 --> 00:23:41,380
巨大宇宙家谱中的一员
of a giant family tree of cosmoses.

432
00:23:41,490 --> 00:23:44,480
由于我们看不到宇宙外面
Since we can't see outside our universe,

433
00:23:44,590 --> 00:23:47,750
这一想法可能还很难得到证实
this idea will likely remain hard to prove,

434
00:23:47,860 --> 00:23:50,750
但还有一种方法
but there is one other way

435
00:23:50,860 --> 00:23:54,290
可判断宇宙是否活着
we could determine whether the universe is alive.

436
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:57,000
我们可以寻找它的大脑
We could find its brain.

437
00:24:01,070 --> 00:24:04,270
它用什么来思考？
What does it take to think?

438
00:24:04,620 --> 00:24:09,910
我们的大脑中 数十亿嘈杂的神经元组成网络
Inside my head is a network of several billion buzzing neurons.

439
00:24:10,020 --> 00:24:11,440
电脑中
In a computer,

440
00:24:11,560 --> 00:24:16,820
电子脉冲在迷宫般的微电路中疾驰
electrical pulses scurry across a maze of microscopic circuits.

441
00:24:16,930 --> 00:24:22,220
现在 科学家超越原子而构建电脑
Now some scientists are building computers out of atoms,

442
00:24:22,330 --> 00:24:26,930
通过量子力学定律来驱动逻辑
deriving logic from the laws of quantum mechanics.

443
00:24:27,040 --> 00:24:31,500
鉴于这些定律适用于整个宇宙
Since those laws apply to the entire universe,

444
00:24:31,610 --> 00:24:35,470
宇宙缘何不能思考？
why couldn't the universe think?

445
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:39,340
在麻省理工学院 赛斯·劳埃德
At M.I.T., Seth Lloyd welcomes us

446
00:24:39,450 --> 00:24:43,740
欢迎我们走进量子计算这一奇异而美妙的世界
into the weird and wondrous world of quantum computing.

447
00:24:43,860 --> 00:24:46,580
这台机器计算的方法
This is a machine that calculates in a way

448
00:24:46,690 --> 00:24:50,820
将在未来取代我们现有的电脑
that will one day leave our current computers in the dust.

449
00:24:50,930 --> 00:24:52,380
它基于一个想法
It is based on the idea

450
00:24:52,500 --> 00:24:58,090
即原子和每个亚原子粒子可以思考
that atoms and every subatomic particle can think.

451
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:00,590
每个原子 每个基本粒子
Every atom, every elementary particle

452
00:25:00,710 --> 00:25:02,330
储存着数比特信息
stores bits of information.

453
00:25:02,440 --> 00:25:04,830
例如这样的电子自旋为0
So electrons spinning like that is 0,

454
00:25:04,940 --> 00:25:06,730
这种电子自旋为1
electrons spinning like that is 1.

455
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:09,630
当两个粒子碰撞
And every time two particles collide,

456
00:25:09,750 --> 00:25:12,570
这些信息位翻转并被处理
those bits get flipped and processed.

457
00:25:12,680 --> 00:25:15,640
量子计算机能力的关键
The key to a quantum computer's power

458
00:25:15,750 --> 00:25:19,010
在于它可以同时思考不止一件事
is that it can think more than one thing at a time.

459
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:21,850
为了理解缘何如此
But to understand how that can be,

460
00:25:21,960 --> 00:25:23,850
先要领会
we need to comprehend

461
00:25:23,960 --> 00:25:27,020
量子力学这一奇怪的科学
the strange science of quantum mechanics.

462
00:25:27,130 --> 00:25:29,590
关于量子力学，要记住的关键在于
The key thing to remember about quantum mechanics

463
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:31,560
量子力学太诡异了
is that quantum mechanics is weird.

464
00:25:31,670 --> 00:25:33,160
这个科技术语
This is a technical term.

465
00:25:33,270 --> 00:25:35,430
意思是违背直觉，潮到爆
It means funky, counterintuitive.

466
00:25:35,540 --> 00:25:38,140
就像科学中的詹姆斯·布朗
It's like the James Brown of sciences.

467
00:25:38,810 --> 00:25:42,800
你不知道接下来会发生什么
You don't know what's going on or what's gonna happen next.

468
00:25:42,910 --> 00:25:44,870
至少一百年来
For the last 100 years,

469
00:25:44,980 --> 00:25:48,340
科学家从微观层面来研究自然
as scientists have studied nature at the microscopic level,

470
00:25:48,450 --> 00:25:50,880
他们意识到一个微观粒子确实
they have noticed that particles can actually exist

471
00:25:50,990 --> 00:25:53,310
可以同时在不同地方存在
in more than one place at the same time.

472
00:25:53,420 --> 00:25:57,650
当他们纠结于如何解释为何如此之时
And while they have struggled to explain why this should happen,

473
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:00,590
赛斯和量子计算领域的同事
Seth and his colleagues in quantum computing

474
00:26:00,700 --> 00:26:04,160
只是简单的接受了它，并利用它
have simply accepted it and used it to their advantage.

475
00:26:04,270 --> 00:26:07,490
现在我将要用这些轻量级的精致设备
I will now use this sophisticated lite-brite device

476
00:26:07,610 --> 00:26:11,160
来演示量子计算机与传统计算机之间
to demonstrate the difference between classical computers

477
00:26:11,280 --> 00:26:12,660
的区别
and quantum computers.

478
00:26:12,780 --> 00:26:15,730
在计算机中，传统的或者量子的
So in any computer, classical or quantum,

479
00:26:15,850 --> 00:26:18,740
可以用此处的一个电子 表示一比特
a bit can be represented by electron over here.

480
00:26:18,850 --> 00:26:21,140
现在，传统计算机
Now, in a classical computer,

481
00:26:21,250 --> 00:26:30,010
电子在这里，0，或者在那里，1
the electron is either over here, 0, or over there, 1.

482
00:26:30,130 --> 00:26:33,920
同一时间绝不会同时是0和1
It is definitely not 0 and 1 at the same time.

483
00:26:34,030 --> 00:26:36,320
但在量子计算机中
But in a quantum computer,

484
00:26:36,430 --> 00:26:41,160
电子非常乐于同时在0和1两个地方
electrons very happily are both 0 and 1 at the same time.

485
00:26:41,270 --> 00:26:43,590
这会对计算带来的影响
To see how this affects computation,

486
00:26:43,710 --> 00:26:49,430
假设这里的电子说，“这样，
let's suppose that electron over here says, "do this.

487
00:26:49,550 --> 00:26:51,470
让其他所有的比特
"Let's tell all these other bits

488
00:26:51,580 --> 00:26:56,110
一同摆出有趣的图案”
to make some interesting pattern."

489
00:26:56,220 --> 00:26:58,810
我们
And now you see what we have

490
00:26:58,920 --> 00:27:00,980
不能仅用一比特
is we don't just have one bit

491
00:27:01,090 --> 00:27:04,350
同时完成这一切
that is doing this and that at the same time,

492
00:27:04,460 --> 00:27:07,850
我们有许许多多比特
we have many, many bits

493
00:27:07,970 --> 00:27:12,490
它们同时完成这一切
that are doing this and that at the same time.

494
00:27:12,600 --> 00:27:16,030
而量子计算机可以利用某些炫到爆的量子状态
So the quantum computer is doing two computations

495
00:27:16,140 --> 00:27:19,300
同时执行两条指令
in some funky quantum sense at the same time.

496
00:27:19,410 --> 00:27:22,300
然后，嘿，没理由就此罢手
And, hey, there's no reason to stop there.

497
00:27:22,410 --> 00:27:25,170
量子计算机完全乐于
Quantum computer is completely happy

498
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:28,410
搞定成百上千，数十亿或更多的
doing 100 or 1,000 or a billion

499
00:27:28,520 --> 00:27:31,080
或这和宇宙中基本粒子数目一样多的
or as many things as there are elementary particles

500
00:27:31,190 --> 00:27:34,180
工作，并同时完成
in the universe, all at the same time.

501
00:27:34,290 --> 00:27:36,150
越多越好
The more, the merrier.

502
00:27:36,260 --> 00:27:39,250
赛斯用了超过二十年的时间
Seth has spent the last two decades

503
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:42,650
来安排这些有序的量子比特
making this ordered rearrangement of quantum bits

504
00:27:42,770 --> 00:27:45,390
在一个真实的
work in a real device,

505
00:27:45,500 --> 00:27:50,290
一个可用来计算的设备中工作
one that can do real calculations.

506
00:27:50,410 --> 00:27:52,460
量子计算机的心脏
The heart of this quantum computer

507
00:27:52,580 --> 00:27:54,200
冷冻在液态氦中
is chilled with liquid helium

508
00:27:54,310 --> 00:27:57,030
由称作“库比特”的
and consists of a small, superconducting

509
00:27:57,150 --> 00:27:59,770
微型超导电子线路组成
electrical circuit known as a cubit.

510
00:27:59,880 --> 00:28:02,840
“库比特”，这些小东西
This cubit is a little, tiny thing.

511
00:28:02,950 --> 00:28:07,150
位于在这样的金封装中
It sits in a tiny little gold package like this,

512
00:28:07,260 --> 00:28:10,550
在其中间 有一个微型电路
and in the middle of it, there's a tiny little circuit

513
00:28:10,660 --> 00:28:13,920
超导电流可以永远这样流动
which a supercurrent can go around like this forever,

514
00:28:14,030 --> 00:28:16,490
或者永远那样流动
or it can go around like that forever.

515
00:28:16,600 --> 00:28:20,930
我们认定这样的超导电流是0
So we call supercurrent going around like this 0

516
00:28:21,040 --> 00:28:24,830
而这样的超导电流是1
and supercurrent going around like that 1

517
00:28:24,940 --> 00:28:28,370
而同时的 这样和那样流动的超导电流
and supercurrent going around this way and that way

518
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:31,370
同时代表0和1
at the same time 0 and 1 at the same time.

519
00:28:31,480 --> 00:28:34,910
这一架子昂贵的设备
All this rack of expensive equipment here

520
00:28:35,020 --> 00:28:37,540
被设计用来和库比特交流
is designed to talk to the cubit

521
00:28:37,660 --> 00:28:40,510
通过给它发射微波信号
by sending microwave signals to it,

522
00:28:40,620 --> 00:28:42,450
来给它搔痒、按摩
to tickle it and massage it.

523
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:46,180
我作为量子力学工程师
My job as a quantum mechanical engineer

524
00:28:46,300 --> 00:28:49,890
就像量子原子的按摩师
is kind of like a quantum atomic masseur.

525
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:53,960
我们通过各种不同方式按摩这些分子
We massage these molecules in a bunch of different ways.

526
00:28:54,070 --> 00:28:56,590
如果你恰当地按摩这些分子
And if you massage the molecule the right way,

527
00:28:56,710 --> 00:28:58,730
会得到这样的摇摆和振动
you can get this wiggling and jiggling

528
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:01,270
这正它们是在计算
so that it's actually performing a computation.

529
00:29:01,380 --> 00:29:04,470
这些分子会放松，并说
So the molecule, as it relaxes and sighs,

530
00:29:04,580 --> 00:29:05,770
“哦，感觉爽到爆，
"oh, that feels so good,"

531
00:29:05,880 --> 00:29:09,270
你要真让我算3乘以5
